The modules' alembic branch (label `modules`), issue #89.

There is no env.py here: backend/app/db/alembic.ini lists this directory's
`versions/` as a second version location, and the core env.py runs the
migrations of both chains.  alembic skips the entry when `modules/` is absent,
so a core checkout sees one head and a full checkout two -- name them
plurally (`alembic upgrade heads`, `alembic stamp heads`).  The entry is
anchored at the .ini file's own directory, so every command works from any
working directory; it used to be relative to the working directory, and from
`backend/` alembic silently skipped this branch entirely.

The first revision, modules_0001_rbac, is a child of the core revision
a7b8c9d0e1f2 -- `down_revision`, not `depends_on`.  It used to be an
independent alembic base, and alembic was then free to schedule it before the
core revision whose work it undoes; `depends_on` is not the repair either,
because it removes the depended-on revision from `heads` and a core image then
cannot resolve the single row a full bootstrap wrote.  So the core chain grew a
second child of the same revision (b8c9d0e1f2a3) and the ordering comes from
ancestry.  The revision's own docstring has the whole argument.  Later module
revisions chain from it.  Generate one from anywhere with

    alembic -c backend/app/db/alembic.ini revision --autogenerate --head modules@head -m "..."

which lands the file here.  A core revision names the core head instead
(`--head <id from alembic heads>`) and lands in
backend/app/db/migrations/versions.

Rolling back: `alembic downgrade modules@-1`, because a bare `-1` is ambiguous
with two heads.  NOT `modules@base` -- this is not an alembic base any more,
and with a single tree root alembic cannot filter a downgrade by branch label,
so `modules@base` resolves to 26 revisions: the whole core chain to base, every
table dropped.  modules_0001_rbac's downgrade drops only the objects its own
upgrade() created -- it marks each one with a PostgreSQL COMMENT -- so it never
drops the tables a fresh full-profile bootstrap built from the models.

Moving to the core profile: nothing to do.  `db/bootstrap.py` leaves the rows
of this branch alone and skips alembic when the core chain is already at its
head; see the "Switching Profile" section of docs/self-hosting/migrations.md.
